    "evidence_text": "We found significant differences on the distribution of followers, the number of URLs on tweets, and the verification of the users.\n\nAs such, our paper aims to\n\nThe data collection ran for just one day (Nov 8th 2016).\n\nThis annotated dataset  is publicly available and can be freely reused.\n\nThe following results detail characteristics of these tweets along the previously mentioned dimensions.\n\nThat is confirmed by the Kolmogorov-Smirnoff test, which does not discard the hypothesis that\n\nthe associated distributions are equal.\n\nFigure FIGREF26 shows the distribution of favourites.\n\nDespite the apparent visual differences, the difference are not statistically significant.\n\nFigure FIGREF27 shows the density distribution of the number of hashtags used.\n\nHowever, the density distribution of friends from the accounts (Figure FIGREF29 ) shows that there is indeed a statistically significant difference in their distributions.\n\nIn fact, such differences are statistically significant.\n\nFigures FIGREF31 and FIGREF32 show this representation.\n\nThe distribution of those accounts not generating fake news is more evenly distributed.\n\nHowever, the difference is not statistically significant.\n\nIn fact, the difference between the two distributions is statistically significant (assuming INLINEFORM0 ).",
